DTLS fingerprint comparison and certificate verification for libpeer (extern/libpeer).

extern/libpeer is a git SUBMODULE, so its sources are not edited in place:
the next `git submodule update` would throw the changes away.  The fixes
therefore live as a patch file next to the script (tools/libpeer-switch/
*.patch) and are applied to the checked out tree right before libpeer is
built.  That keeps the engine patch self contained: one patch file for the
engine, one patch file for the submodule.

This is the sixth patch of that series.  It is applied AFTER
0001-switch-webrtc-fixes, 0002-no-stdout-log, 0003-h265-depacketizer,
0004-dtls-diagnostics and 0005-ice-roles-prflx-and-timing.

Why
---
The DTLS handshake completes and then libpeer throws the peer away because
the certificate does not "match" the fingerprint the offer announced:

  * The fingerprint was compared as a STRING (strncmp() in
    dtls_srtp_handshake()).  libwebrtc prints a fingerprint in UPPER case
    hex (SSLFingerprint::GetRfc4572Fingerprint() hex encodes the digest and
    upper cases it) and it compares the DIGEST BYTES
    (SSLFingerprint::operator==), not the printed string.  A peer that
    prints its fingerprint in lower case therefore never matched: the
    handshake succeeded and the correct certificate was rejected right
    after it.

  * The certificate verification was stricter than the official client
    (BoringSSL with SSL_VERIFY_PEER and its own verify callback).  The
    official client only checks the fingerprint digest that the SDP
    announced -- it does NOT check the CA chain and it does NOT check the
    validity period.  libpeer cleared NOT_TRUSTED / CN_MISMATCH / BAD_KEY
    but NOT BADCERT_EXPIRED / BADCERT_FUTURE, so a clock that is a little
    off on the Switch broke the handshake.

What it changes
---------------
1. src/dtls_srtp.c -- the fingerprint is compared on its BYTES:

     dtls_srtp_x509_digest_bytes()    the SHA-256 digest of the certificate
                                      as 32 raw bytes
     dtls_srtp_x509_digest()          the same digest, printed (unchanged)
     dtls_srtp_fingerprint_to_bytes() "AB:CD:EF:..." -> 32 raw bytes
     dtls_srtp_bytes_equal()          constant time comparison
     dtls_srtp_fingerprint_matches()  the verdict (1 / 0 / -1)

   The decoder takes the value the way the peer may print it: upper case or
   lower case hex, ":" or no separator, with or without the algorithm token
   ("sha-256 ").  A value that cannot be parsed -- a wrong length, a stray
   character, a dangling half byte -- is a MISMATCH and is logged; it is
   never silently accepted.

   The verdict semantics of 0035 are unchanged: fingerprint_check is 1 when
   the fingerprints match, -1 when they do not match (or cannot be parsed)
   and -2 when the peer sent no certificate at all.

2. src/dtls_srtp.c -- the verify callback (dtls_srtp_cert_verify()) clears
   two more flags:

     MBEDTLS_X509_BADCERT_EXPIRED   the certificate is not valid any more
     MBEDTLS_X509_BADCERT_FUTURE    the certificate is not valid yet

   Both are behind #ifdef: MBEDTLS_X509_BADCERT_FUTURE does not exist in
   mbedTLS 2.x (the tree is built against 3.4.0, but config.h still has the
   CONFIG_MBEDTLS_2_X branch), so the macro is used only when the header
   defines it.

   The alternative -- MBEDTLS_SSL_VERIFY_NONE -- is NOT used here: it would
   switch the certificate verification off COMPLETELY and leave the SDP
   fingerprint as the only guard.  Clearing the two flags keeps the
   verification in place and is the smaller, reversible change.

3. src/config.h -- NOT touched: the DTLS certificate is an ECDSA P-256 key
   already.  0005-ice-roles-prflx-and-timing.patch sets
   CONFIG_DTLS_USE_ECDSA to 1 and checks the errors of mbedtls_pk_setup() /
   mbedtls_ecp_gen_key(), so this patch does not repeat that work.

No key material is logged or exported by this patch.
diff --git a/src/dtls_srtp.c b/src/dtls_srtp.c
